rgins":null,"_gp_margins":null,"_score_margins":null,"_gate_fund_growth":null,"_gp_fund_growth":null,"_score_fund_growth":null,"_gate_shrhldr_yield":0.04189274984351023,"_gp_shrhldr_yield":true,"_score_shrhldr_yield":57.7,"_gate_share_shrink":0.0068055335075438705,"_gp_share_shrink":false,"_score_share_shrink":41.5,"_gate_sbc_dilution":0.0021819763167934955,"_gp_sbc_dilution":true,"_score_sbc_dilution":96.4,"_gate_insider_own":0.64309996,"_gp_insider_own":true,"_score_insider_own":100.0,"_gate_insider_buying":0.0,"_gp_insider_buying":false,"_score_insider_buying":0.0,"pf":[],"description_full":"Atlanticus Holdings Corporation, a financial technology company, provides products and services to lenders in the United States. The company operates in two segments, Credit as a Service (CaaS) and Auto Finance. Its CaaS segment offers private label credit products associated with the healthcare space under the Curae brand, as well as consumer electronics, furniture, elective medical procedures, and home-improvement under the Fortiva brand and its retail partners' brands; and general-purpose credit cards under the Aspire, Imagine, Mercury, and Fortiva brand names. The company's private label and general-purpose credit cards originated from its bank partners through various channels, including retail and healthcare point-of-sale locations, direct mail solicitation, and digital marketing and partnerships with third parties. This segment also offers loan servicing, such as risk management and customer service outsourcing for third parties, as well as engages in other product testing and investments. The Auto Finance segment purchases and/or services loans secured by automobiles from or for a pre-qualified network of independent automotive dealers and automotive finance companies in the buy-here and pay-here used car business. This segment also provides floor plan financing and installment lending products. The company was founded in 1996 and is headquartered in Atlanta, Georgia.","ceo_bio":"Mr. Jeffrey A. Howard | President, CEO & Director | Age 55 | Compensation: $1.9M (FY2025)","culture_narrative":"Employs approximately 576 people. Revenue per employee of $3.4M ranks in the top quartile among Financial Services peers, improving at 20%/yr \u2014 growing workforce leverage. Stock-based compensation of $7K per employee reflects an ownership culture. CEO compensation is 1\u00d7 revenue per employee (modest relative to workforce productivity). Governance score flags elevated compensation governance risk (9/10).","financial_summary":["Atlanticus Holdings Corporation is a small-cap financial services business with a $1.4B market cap on $2.0B of annual revenue.","The top line has compounded at +23% over the past three years and +42% annually across the full decade, the kind of sustained demand persistence that marks a compounder.","Returns on capital run at 44% against a 12% cost of capital \u2014 a 32-point spread that is the clearest signature of an economic moat.","On the balance sheet, the balance sheet carries net cash, which is the gold standard: the business never has to make desperate decisions in a bad year, and thin 0.5\u00d7 interest coverage leaves no room for a bad year.","Shareholder yield of 4.2% reflects a genuine commitment to returning capital rather than empire-building, and insiders hold 64% of the shares, so management thinks like owners because it is one \u2014 the strongest alignment there is, and profit capture is only 0.3\u00d7 the fair share, suggesting no structural edge inside its industry."],"sector_headwinds":[{"text":"Piotroski score 3/9 \u2014 the fundamentals are deteriorating on multiple fronts","sev":"red","cat":"quality"},{"text":"Altman Z in the distress zone (0.5) \u2014 the kind of balance-sheet risk that can make a loss permanent","sev":"red","cat":"quality"},{"text":"Very elevated short interest (32.1% of float) \u2014 sophisticated money is betting against the business","sev":"red","cat":"sentiment"},{"t